DreamFactory development engine
==============================

This is an experimental engine for Titanic (DreamFactory 4) and Dust
(DreamFactory 1). Normal launch runs BOOTFILE and authored input, story,
dialogue, inventory, media and timers. The supplied English Windows retail,
September 1996 interactive demo and Dust pass fresh-start completion paths on
one native build. Hash-verified checkpoint chains contain 57 retail traces,
2 demo traces and 330 Dust traces. Retail reaches its four-evidence best
ending, credits and quit; Dust completes both gunfights, the duel, four temples,
Bloodstone and the Yunni ending/credits quit. Separate regressions cover
alternate endings, settings, native save/load, invalid saves and death recovery.
These checks do not exhaust every dialogue/quest permutation or establish
support for other editions. Original TI/DF saves cannot be imported, and
saving during modal movies/dialogue/camera actions is disabled.
dreamfactory_viewer=true explicitly selects the developer room viewer.
Older progress entries below are historical implementation milestones.

Current music uses one shared queued channel with authored repeat roots.
Repeated banks have independent controls and owners. New checkpoints are DFSV
v8; v1-v7 remain readable. Dust separates one-shots from music and preserves
duplicate one-shot records. The workspace's build/completion-requirements.json
records current validation scope and evidence.

Format and codec reference:
  https://github.com/dhobi/dreamrefactory
  revision d08950b58191fd20e22d2184d7334afbe737bb1c
  GPL-3.0, dreamREfactory contributors, with format work credited there to DFET.

image.cpp adapts engine/src/df/image.ts's frame decoder. archive.cpp follows
container.ts's envelope layout; room.cpp and room_v1.cpp follow the documented
SET layouts in set.ts and set-v1.ts. Frame decoding also retains the optional
RLE scenery depth plane, with bounded row offsets and exact row coverage.
A frame without depth clears the previous depth plane; malformed input leaves
both colour and depth untouched. Room readers retain v4 motion camera coordinates,
view heights, depth quantization, and Dust's eye height and camera setback.
Independent reference hashes match all 37,452 decoded depth planes in the
122 preserved SET files, including original disc variants. The C++ readers add explicit bounds and
reference checks and use ScummVM types. All these files are GPL-3.0-or-later.

script.cpp adapts df/script.ts and runtime/parser.ts; runtime.cpp follows
runtime/interp.ts. stage.cpp follows df/stg.ts's v1/v4 layouts. opcodes.h is
derived from df/opcodes.ts, which credits DFET (M3tox) for the opcode names.
session.cpp implements boot library loading and scalar host commands, following
runtime/builtins. motion.cpp follows runtime/scheduler.ts's v1/v4 actor
walk/turn records and completion dispatch. stars.cpp reads primary/secondary
placements and routes from df/set.ts and df/set-v1.ts. Session room entry binds
those tables and settles pending actors. resources.cpp resolves script paths against preserved installed
and CD trees; it never flattens canonical resource lookup across discs.
The separate reference audits execute those TypeScript modules unchanged.

rooms.cpp follows runtime/setscripts.ts and web/viewer.ts: stable room/scene/
painting scripts, lifecycle chains, scene timers, authored navigation, named
jumps, re-entry and callback-driven replacement. room_v1.cpp reads the full
scene table, including built-on cells and custom names, and its trailing script
references. All 122 preserved SET files match reference room/camera metadata hashes; native
checks reconstruct every available standpoint. Synthetic v1/v4 tests cover
containment, stage-library resolution, keyboard forwarding, self-closing scripts,
Dust grid queries, malformed references/counts/names, delta images and zero-image
scripted scenes. SDL checks cover palette/scaled output, pacing and quit during
a scripted move. Depth planes, projected actors/props and native input routing
are connected in gameplay; the developer viewer is selected explicitly.

audio.cpp adapts df/audio.ts, banks.ts and snd.ts for V40/V41 audio and bank
layouts. sound.cpp supplies ScummVM mixer streams for music, effects and speech;
session.cpp exposes the corresponding script calls. PCM and resampled music
hashes match the reference for all supplied banks. Mixer host smoke tests use
SDL dummy audio. Scene audio is connected; playback save state remains pending.

cast.cpp adapts df/cst.ts and the transparent sprite codec in df/shp.ts. Cast
metadata, playback lists, angle variants, sprite anchors, palette indexes and
opacity masks match the reference throughout the supplied CST corpus. actors.cpp
connects cast lifecycle events, copies, deletion, enumeration and state storage
to the session, following runtime/actors.ts and session-resources.ts. Deferred
actor/cast dispatch and containment contexts follow session-events.ts. Compiled
v1/v4 fixtures check dispatch, fresh copy state, scriptless actors and storage
lifetime when a handler closes its cast. Animation, projection and stage composition are connected. Timed walking, turning
and star routes are implemented in motion.cpp, with session room binding. Actual Dust BOOTFILE creates 16 actor copies through openactor handlers.

scheduler.cpp follows runtime/scheduler.ts and clock.ts: the 32-entry one-shot
loop table, case-folded identity, ordered replacement, countdown suspension and
50 ms service/60 Hz tick clocks. Session timing calls drive actor callbacks and
cooperative yields without re-entering the same handler. The actor audit also
checks loop edge cases and compiled delay/forceupdate fixtures with virtual and
real SDL clocks. Scene/flat dispatch, walking, positional ambience and BOOT idle
are connected in gameplay; the separate room viewer does not run the session.

crickets.cpp follows runtime/scheduler.ts: Titanic's 16-entry room-scoped ambience
table with independent countdown jitter, fixed coordinates, pause and one-shot
semantics. sound.cpp tracks positional mixer handles, sharing retail's two SFX
slots with ordinary effects, and applies positional gain/pan
and wave-master scaling. Synthetic compiled APIs and SDL mixer tests cover
capacity/replacement, busy countdowns, overlapping loops, room exit/return, bank
closure and reset. Integer distance/volume and folded bearing pan now follow
the supplied original executables; active loops follow listener movement and
stop outside their audible radius. Session rooms bind the room and settled camera
listener; the ordinary interactive viewer still does not run that session.

movie.cpp follows df/mov.ts, mov-v1.ts and the reference movie player's action
model. It reads segmented Windows v1/v4 movies and retains separate raw delta
and Dust transparency-composited frames. All 535 Windows movie files match
the reference metadata, indexed pixel and PCM hashes. Eleven unused Macintosh
assets in Dust's ZUNUSED directory are identified by their big-endian size field
and excluded. Synthetic native checks cover malformed references and cycles,
frame timing, voice waits, regions, cues, sound-follow jumps, chained calls and
returns, action flags and skips. The MovieHost interface separates platform
operations from the tested controller. movie_host.cpp connects ScummVM display
and mixer operations to modal session playmovie/actionframe calls. Compiled
fixtures check actual indexed screen output, palettes, sound/bed separation,
segment inheritance, narration tails/replacement, voice waits, stale input,
clicks, Escape, reset flags and quit abandoning the script. Real BOOTFILE diagnostics now complete startup for both Titanic editions:
the demo reaches demo.stg/demo 1; retail reaches bedsit1 Scene2/View14 and
main.stg/main 1 after the authored game/tour menu. Dust completes its three
intro movies and opens Town Scene G15/north with new.flt/mainpanel, returning
normally. Dust has two casts/54 actors (16 copies) and two shops/73 props
(six copies) at startup. Native conversation rendering is connected to session diagnostics; native player input is connected in experimental session mode. Normal launch runs the authored session.

shop.cpp adapts df/shp.ts's v1/v4 SHP/PRP directory, state and play-list reader,
including its structural idle-pose correction. Prop sprites share cast.cpp's
transparent codec. All 61 supplied shops match metadata and sprite hashes;
synthetic checks cover repeated steps, degree selectors, reference-scale
defaults, non-drawing archive gaps, malformed references/counts and name lengths.
SCORP's desk references a typed archive gap, which is an empty picture. Shop
lifecycle and basic prop host commands are connected by props.cpp and session.cpp.
They fire openprop before openshop, preserve state and re-fire openshop on
re-entry, and fire closeshop before removal. Deferred prop/shop dispatch, copies,
state accessors and prop loop callbacks have compiled v1/v4 fixtures. Visibility
hiding is separate from the visible flag; compiled checks verify visible-only
broadcasts, newly shown controls, named hiding, release and reset. Screen-space animation, rendering and hit testing are connected. World
projection and gameplay input are connected to stage composition. Existence
queries remain pending. Named/remembered prop-star
placement and deferred settlement on room entry are connected; compiled checks
cover room filtering, explicit positions, facing overrides, copies and defaults.

cursors.cpp reads CURS.<name> groups from the user's original executable via
ScummVM's WinResources/WinCursorGroup readers. All 31 named cursor images and
hotspots match dreamREfactory's independent dumpcursors.ts reader. Nested
hide/show counters, case folding, NONE, fallback and reset have compiled checks.
The data preparation script retains TI.EXE/DF.EXE as resource archives only.

Session CLUT commands and blocking fades retain source palettes separately
from black, use one 60 Hz tick per ramp step, and honor quit while deferring
input and leaving the scheduler frozen. SDL and virtual-clock fixtures cover
named palettes, range mixing, endpoints, timing and script abandonment. Room
rendering retains a black palette across a draw until explicitly revealed.
Plain visualeffect preserves black; named puppet palettes are available from
the loaded conversation. Animated effects and persistent per-layer dimming
still require the compositor.

Static analysis of the supplied executables also informs session.cpp:
  DF.EXE SHA256 d8a22e266c7307e550bd7284d2743947765303348a6b33cda48534c467aa95fe
  TI.EXE (retail) SHA256 09e4c9c6536660c303fd26f0fa01dc91e2583eb78b81243f8fb1b27ef761011f
Dust actorhitbox setter/getter 0x4261a0/0x4262b0 store signed words and a
separate player pair. Actor collision remains pending. Distance routines
DF.EXE 0x405e00 and TI.EXE 0x4276a0 sign-extend packed halves, wrap the squared
sum at 32 bits, and return a truncated unsigned word square root, unlike the
reference's rounded floating-point result. Camera-height getter/setter in this
retail build (0x427b30/0x43dc60) read/write a signed word. Compiled checks cover
negative coordinates, extreme-word distance arithmetic and fresh actor copies.
No original executable instruction bytes are included in engine sources.

puppet.cpp follows df/pup.ts: v1's direct stance versus v4's directory,
eleven layer tables, dialogue/subtitle records, speech/animation references,
per-line stance selection, the answer band and four idle intervals. All 95
supplied Windows PUP files match independent metadata, sprite, lip-sync and
PCM hashes; every linked script parses. Synthetic boundary cases cover counts,
references, names, invalid stances and transactional animation failure.
puppets.cpp and Session connect loading, internal names/script enumeration,
branch dispatch/containment, base/default poses, visibility, five choice slots
and the original random-swap scramble. Compiled tests cover context, passcode,
missing/invalid opens, self-close/replacement lifetime and choice behavior.
PuppetSpeech follows runtime/puppet.ts's line timing, caption gate, stance and
lip-sync playback. The modal Session host decodes PUP audio into the voice
channel, waits PCM duration plus 150 ms, consumes Escape across the whole speech
run, records up to three repeat lines, ignores clicks and handles Ctrl+digits
for volume. Missing audio falls back to text-byte length with a 1 s minimum.
Compiled SDL and deterministic checks cover timing, poses, caption suppression,
queue caps, interrupted runs, replacement lifetime, malformed animation,
independent voice/effect channels and quit abandoning the waiting script.
PuppetChoices and Session implement the modal puppetevent row tracker, ten-tick
press floor, drag cancellation, retained choices and Escape's -1 branch.
Picture clicks replay the prior answered plaque and up to three queued replies
without re-entering the script; answer-to-voice matching uses text, not idents.
Parameter 8 enables the four authored idle intervals. Idle timer draws use the
original shared script RNG, rather than the reference browser's deliberate
ambient-stream deviation. Idle lines use blocking speech, discard mouse input
and exit the plaque on Escape with the interrupt flag intact. Silent-plaque
Escape does not suppress the script's following parting line. Native/compiled
checks cover these paths and quit-state isolation between test sessions.
Native conversation display is connected to session speech and answer waits;
normal gameplay uses the authored session.

puppet_view.cpp follows web/puppet-view.ts for art composition: a 512x384
logical screen in both supplied formats, eleven anchored sprite layers, matte
room backgrounds, subtitle clipping and the original five-row answer band.
Room and puppet palettes stay separate in the RGBA canvas. Independent audits
execute the actual reference compositor with neutral gamma and compare images
for represented stances, opening/final poses, captions and changing backdrops
across all 95 PUP files. Native boundary fixtures cover layer order, opaque zero,
reserved endpoints, anchors, transparency, band clipping, visibility, character
replacement and fixed half-open choice rectangles. The native RGB display now presents this canvas during session conversation
waits; normal gameplay input routing is connected.

PuppetView's text overlay follows the original Windows caption and plaque
layout documented in web/puppet-view.ts, including literal-space wrapping at
496 pixels, a two-line ceiling, baseline spacing, separate subtitle/answer
margins, raw CLUT inks, and three-pixel selected/inverted frames. CP1252 is
converted to Unicode before measuring/drawing. The unbroken overlong caption
case keeps the original's no-text behavior, not the browser CJK deviation.
PuppetFonts uses ScummVM's FreeType interface, cached by signed size, and owns
each lazy font stream. A malformed replacement preserves loaded fonts. Native
fixtures check layout and stream lifetime; previews combine real supplied
portraits, subtitle text and answer rows. Session speech and answer waits present the canvas through the native RGB
display; the normal interactive entry point runs the authored session.

The Windows build pins FreeType 2.13.3 and Liberation Sans 2.1.5 via
SHA256-checked archives in tools/prepare-fonts.ps1. Font bytes can instead come
from a supplied Arial file. Liberation Sans aims for Arial-compatible metrics
and is not pixel-identical to Arial. Build font assets and their OFL/FreeType
license notices are kept in build/runtime-data, outside proprietary game data.
Engine configure dependencies now include highres, 16bit and freetype2.

DreamCatcher was reviewed for structural/string background, without copying code:
  https://github.com/JamesK89/DreamCatcher

Data: tools/prepare-data.py preserves installed and CD resource trees, including
all puzzle, inventory, puppet and movie directories. It also writes a flat view
for the development room viewer. Differing same-name disc resources must remain
available to the volume-aware session resolver. Script path slot 0 is read-only;
slots 1..8 are searched in order. Virtual paths are case insensitive.
Detection currently covers only the supplied English Windows editions.

Controls: Left/Right turn, Up walks, PgUp/PgDn switch SET, Tab changes scene,
Escape exits. No scripts or actors are run. Room boundaries do not invoke scripts.

display.cpp shares indexed room/movie and RGB conversation presentation.
It preserves separate room/character palettes, black holds, picture fades and
world restoration. Physical-screen checks cover scaling, text/press feedback,
palette mixing, restoration, and both movie formats after RGB promotion.
Each supplied edition plays a short authored voiced line and captures the
640x480 native screen to build/puppet-session-<edition>/display-preview.rgba.
These are diagnostic scenes with a synthetic backdrop and answer plaque.
Use tools/preview-rgba.py to export their PNG previews.

stage_view.cpp decodes and caches STG/FLT art by loaded stage ownership and
composes it beneath the visible room on the 512x384 logical screen. Stage and
room palettes stay independent, including during mixclut edits. An uncovered
matte interface retains the prior framebuffer, while conversation composition
still uses its band. Reference comparisons cover 1,635 pictures across all 75
supplied stage files; native checks cover scaling, flat changes, visibility,
independent palettes, black holds, fades and matte retention. Projected actors/props and depth are rendered. BOOT completion now presents the session screen;
the trace tool captures it to build/boot-<edition>.rgba for visual inspection.

props.cpp now chooses frames by stored degree, variant and group-based play
order. The 50 ms animation service holds v1's last frame and repeats v4 views
with multi-step play lists. Runtime handler serials keep a same-handler propdeg
pick separate from a later propview animation. Screen props draw in stable
back-to-front propdist order, preserving transparent pixels and signed anchors;
stage/room palettes color their shared CLUT halves. Each loaded shop owns its
sprite cache. Native checks cover timing, variants, hidden/visible flags,
clipping, opaque zero, overlap, replacement owners and physical-screen palettes.
Reference frame-choice/sequence hashes and 3,132 cropped compositions match all
61 supplied SHP/PRP files. pointinprop tests the named picture's opaque pixels;
frontmost visible-prop hit-testing is ready for gameplay routing. World actors/props now use projection/depth composition.

Developer configuration keys:
  dreamfactory_font=<file>  explicit TrueType font path; otherwise search Arial
    in game resources, then LiberationSans-Regular.ttf on the search path
  dreamfactory_cursor_audit=true  compare named executable cursor pixels/hotspots
  dreamfactory_cursor_fixtures=<file>  compiled cursor visibility/state calls
  dreamfactory_screen_audit=true  test CLUT, blocking fades, input and quit
  dreamfactory_screen_fixtures=<directory>  synthetic palette/fade fixture root
  dreamfactory_verify=true  decode and hash every authored room sequence, then exit
  dreamfactory_smoke=true   open all rooms and exercise navigation without timing
  dreamfactory_script_audit=true  compare parsed script corpus and run fixtures
  dreamfactory_script_fixtures=<directory>  assembled .dfscript test files
  dreamfactory_room_audit=true    hash room/scene/painting metadata and script refs
  dreamfactory_room_fixtures=<directory>  compiled navigation/lifecycle/display fixtures
  dreamfactory_star_audit=true    hash room star/route tables and test reader bounds
  dreamfactory_stage_audit=true   hash stage metadata and decoded flat images
  dreamfactory_stage_fixtures=<directory>  compiled stage/flat/button lifecycle fixtures
  dreamfactory_resource_audit=true  check volume/path-slot resource lookup
  dreamfactory_resource_fixtures=<file>  generated resource query fixture
  dreamfactory_audio_audit=true   compare sound-bank metadata and PCM hashes
  dreamfactory_audio_fixtures=<file>  compiled audio host smoke-test script
  dreamfactory_cricket_fixtures=<directory>  synthetic ambience API/mixer fixtures
  dreamfactory_cast_audit=true    hash cast metadata and transparent sprites
  dreamfactory_puppet_audit=true  hash conversation metadata/sprites/animation/PCM
  dreamfactory_puppet_fixtures=<directory>  synthetic reader/session/speech fixtures
  dreamfactory_movie_audit=true   hash movie metadata/images/audio and test actions
  dreamfactory_movie_fixtures=<directory>  synthetic modal playback/session fixtures
  dreamfactory_shop_audit=true    hash shop metadata and prop sprites; test reader
  dreamfactory_prop_fixtures=<directory>  compiled shop/prop lifecycle fixtures
  dreamfactory_actor_fixtures=<directory>  synthetic cast/session fixture root
  dreamfactory_boot_trace=true    run BOOTFILE until first unsupported command
  dreamfactory_boot_capture=<file>  export successful startup screen as 640x480 RGBA
  dreamfactory_boot_choose_game=true  click the game region in retail PLAYMODE
Audit modes are intended for dedicated test configurations. Script/room/stage/audio/cast/movie/shop modes
scan preserved volumes and installed resources when available. Their log records
PASS/FAIL; they exit without displaying error dialogs for unattended SDL tests.

geometry.h and the world prop compositor follow runtime/geometry.ts and props.ts:
fixed 2.14 camera projection, frame reference scaling, camera-relative directional
pictures, stable back-to-front sorting, transparent pixels, room/viewport filters,
and shared scenery occlusion for drawing and picking. Session stage composition
renders these before screen props. All 12,528 projected corpus crops match the
reference, including four camera headings, directional/animated selection and
synthetic depth masks. All-angle geometry hashes also cover signed extremes.
Native checks cover visibility, transparent holes, depth
equality, zclip and ground bias. Bare-room world sprites share the stage compositor; complete gameplay validation remains pending.

Dust movement camera interpolation is verified from the supplied DF.EXE
(SHA-256 d8a22e266c7307e550bd7284d2743947765303348a6b33cda48534c467aa95fe),
without executing it. The camera builder at 0x40dd90 starts at the cell centre;
0x40de12 uses frame*256/4 for walks, and 0x40df6e uses frame*64/4 for turns.
0x40e0d7 ends after five frames (0..4). This differs from the reference's
estimated (frame+1)/5. Focal length 310 is installed at 0x40d255/0x40d488.
Compiled native fixtures check motion endpoints, stored signed v4 camera poses,
standpoint selection, focal length, setback and camerahi.

Actor rendering follows runtime/actors.ts: play lists index time steps, and
camera-relative angles select the closest record within a step (first wins ties).
The scale reference is the pose's first record, with a 96 fallback. Loaded casts
own sprite caches. Every coarse 50 ms pass advances visible/hidden actor poses
after coarse script callbacks, including idle poses; repeated play entries hold
pictures. These formats do not dispatch endanim. Screen actors use their own
facing at 1:1, including scale zero. World drawing/picking shares projected masks
and scenery occlusion. actordist's setter stores screen ordering; the getter
follows reference drawability/ground-distance semantics, with a 32000 sentinel.

Reference frame-choice hashes cover all 256 angles and every play step; 7,360
projected/screen crops match all 13 supplied CST files. Native tests cover play
holds, angular ties, empty/fallback poses, hidden animation, room filtering,
scenery equality/bias/zclip, transparency, viewport tests and scale-zero actors.
Physical-screen checks verify Dust's merged actor/prop depth list and the current
v4 reference's actor-then-prop passes, including equal-depth behavior. Native
player input is connected in experimental session mode. Bare-room sprite composition uses the shared renderer.

Bare rooms use the full SET palette and copy their frame into the top of the
512x384 logical screen, leaving unused space black. They share stage composition's
world actor/prop ordering, depth masks and screen sprite pass. RGB bare-room
palette commands redraw the scene, preserving black holds. Native display tests
cover absent stages and unselected flats, actor/prop layers, full palette colors,
dimming, reveal and view visibility; room tests check every physical pixel,
real-clock movement pacing, settled views and quit abandonment.

Normal gameplay runs BOOTFILE and Session::step; dreamfactory_viewer=true
selects the separate developer room viewer. Each pass dispatches a queued key/click through
the boot router, or invokes boot idle (authored forceupdate, cursor and calctime).
Pointer polling yields without recursively dispatching gestures. Overlay keys
prefer the flat's keydown, then the stage main. Movie/conversation waits own their
modal events. Input uses a 32-slot queue, repeat coalescing/overflow eviction, and
filters display notifications. Native fixtures verify routers, pointer state,
press/release, overlays, queue policies, scene/painting/button picking and quit.

calcdeg matches the original v1/v4 integer ratio lookup (TI.EXE 0x444b40,
DF.EXE 0x411d20), separately from the renderer's nearest-angle bearing. Fixtures
read the original table from each supplied executable and compare 4,107 cases.
dreamfactory_boot_steps, dreamfactory_boot_keys (comma separated), and
dreamfactory_boot_click_x/y add unattended post-startup input diagnostics.
dreamfactory_boot_click_movies explicitly lists title movies whose first authored
click region the diagnostic event source should press (for example, date.mov).
Full story, puzzle, inventory and save/load validation remains incomplete.

The supplied demo's native menu click, porthole loop, date-card continuation and
flyby now enter C71 / Scene51 / View63 with main.stg / main 1. Forty session passes
complete without an unsupported command. Message writes diagnostic script text
to ScummVM's log. Movie cleanup detaches the borrowed movie pointer before event
sources are polled; a native fixture checks detached segment observation.


Opening interaction checks: tools/verify-gameplay.ps1 verifies demo bag/key
pickup, inventory selection/examination, return and drag-to-trunk; retail
bedsit turning; and Dust map/inventory round trips, cash-loop text and resumed
room input. The diagnostic actions use normal events and test resulting state.
Named movie skips inject Escape only where allowed by the movie segment.

Visual transitions implement barndooropen/close and wipeleft/right with held
source/destination pictures, modal 60Hz waits, retained input and quit handling.
The arriving picture is composed offscreen before the first strip. Native
physical-pixel and timing checks cover all three editions. Other effects remain
unsupported; plain redraws without lifting a pending black hold.

Script drawstring/stringwidth share Windows-1252 native font rendering. Text
persists over recomposition, replaces an existing same-position/size entry,
clears before a new flat's openflat callback, and is erased where a newly shown
prop covers a field. Native stage checks cover these behaviors. Full story,
puzzle, save/load and original-text-layout validation remains incomplete.


The demo opening trace now covers gramophone horn/drawer controls, watch/map
pickup, walking to the door, and (tools/verify-demo-story.ps1) Smethells's three
opening dialogue branches, the note movie, the door gate and entry into HALLA /
Scene52 / View63. Explicit diagnostic choices use ordinary native press/release
events and require every requested choice to be consumed.
The story helper continues along the port corridor, through its door gate and
into GSTAIR2 / Scene11 / View23. Optional ExtraActions/Choices continue the
verified route without changing game state directly.
tools/verify-demo-mission.ps1 continues upstairs to GSTAIR1 / Scene18 / View38,
speaks with Penny and checks the authored phase=1 assignment state. Its native
trace passes 110 actions and five dialogue choices, returning to the room.
tools/verify-demo-photographer.ps1 verifies returning downstairs, entering the
starboard corridor and Burns's first conversation at B59: 155 actions and eight
choices pass with burnsphase=tryagain and burnslike=1. Diagnostic waitpropview
advances ordinary session passes with a bounded deadline for authored timers.
tools/verify-demo-darkroom.ps1 finds Burns's wife and enters B59 with the white
light off. tools/verify-demo-photograph.ps1 develops the Conkling negative under
the red lamp, through the 15-second and 10-second bath windows, then bags the
good print. Its native trace passes 248 actions and ten dialogue choices with
photo1 owned by Frank and quality value 1. Puzzle state comes from the shipped
scripts, scheduler and normal drag/click input.
tools/verify-demo-completion.ps1 passes the supplied September 1996 demo's
winning story path from startup through delivering the print, Penny's final
dialogue, postdemo.mov (including its authored continuation click), and return
to demo.stg / demo 1 with phase=2 and no room loaded. The native trace passes
306 actions and twelve dialogue choices. Alternate outcomes, save/load and the
retail Titanic/Dust playthroughs remain incomplete.

tools/verify-dust-opening.ps1 verifies Leroy's first conversation and departure
flag: 21 actions and one dialogue choice pass with leroyphase=1, mainpanel
visible and Scene G15 / north restored.
tools/verify-dust-bone.ps1 verifies the dog gate at Scene G12 and the help
character's three-question bone branch from the east-facing viewpoint. Its
native trace passes 31 actions and four dialogue choices with bone/small set
by the original script.

tools/verify-dust-ring.ps1 offers the bone to the dog and receives the ring:
52 actions and five choices pass with phase=2, helpphase=3 and ring ownership.
tools/verify-dust-cards.ps1 picks up the jug, waits for Jones and receives his
cards: 73 actions and six choices pass. Diagnostic waitactorstar observes
normal movement with a bounded deadline; it never relocates actors.
countbevels returns the active puppet's choice count, including hidden choices,
or zero without a puppet. Jones's inventory-offer dialogue needs it. Native
puppet fixtures verify empty/hidden/full/cleared counts in all three editions.
The remaining Dust story and puzzles are still unverified.

tools/verify-dust-saloon.ps1 follows Leroy to the shooting range, checks
leroyphase=88, then opens the Hard Drive door and enters SALLOWER: 107 actions
and six choices pass, including saloonphase=1 and the stored town standpoint.

tools/verify-full-bedsit.ps1 scores all seven London-flat objects through their
close-up movies and triggers the raid. tools/verify-full-boarding.ps1 waits
through the scripted raid and crossing to C73 / Scene51 / View63, mission=1,
phase=0, at 9:30. All 35 actions pass. Diagnostic waitset/waitglobal observe
state with bounded deadlines while advancing normal session passes.
Retail missions beyond this opening remain unverified.

tools/verify-full-steward.ps1 collects the bag/watch, completes Smethells's
conversation and note, receives the map and exits into HALLC / Scene27 /
View35: 61 actions and three choices pass. Absent movie event clips leave
existing audio alone; retail PENOTE names voice cues whose recordings are
already in its bed. Native movie fixtures cover missing cues without stopping
or waiting for an earlier film's audio, in all three editions.

tools/verify-dust-blackjack.ps1 enters the table, declines Jan's invitation and
restores the saloon with unchanged cash: 119 actions and seven choices pass.
PlayHand passes one ordinary $1 hand, Stand, Jan's timed next-bet prompt and
Quit back to the saloon: 125 actions and ten choices. This does not establish
winning enough money for later story progression. Pressing Stand after an already-settled
natural blackjack exposed a nested-reset/sound-bank-lifetime failure; that
rapid-input case remains unresolved. The diagnostic clickifempty guard can
skip a gesture when an observed string global is already nonempty; it never
writes game state. The hand route uses it to wait for Jan after a natural.

Stage containment binds me to the handler's button, flat or stage script,
while target retains the addressed button/flat. Unqualified calls into a
parent flat therefore arm flat timers with the flat's name. This corrects
Dust's Stand -> dealerdraw -> resetgame timer, which previously targeted the
button "stay". Native fixtures cover parent calls, me/target and timer delivery
in all three editions. tools/inspect-stage-context.py reproduces read-only
disassembly, guarded by the supplied originals' SHA-256 hashes: retail TI.EXE
4464a0 constructs the chain, 447710 advances its contexts by 4e, and 40647b /
4064b2 read me/target at context+1e/+3e. Dust DF.EXE 416a80 constructs the same
button/flat/stage context layout. Other object families retain their existing
context behavior pending equivalent original-code evidence.

calcvectx/calcvecty implement the reference's 14-bit fixed-point components:
8-bit heading, signed 16-bit magnitude/result, division toward zero. Native
script fixtures compare all 256 headings, wrapped angles and signed magnitude
boundaries against the primary reference in all editions. Like that reference,
entries are computed from rounded sine/cosine; the original TRIG resources can
differ by one least-significant table unit. tools/inspect-trig.py reads and
hash-checks the supplied originals without executing them: retail/demo RAW
TRIG1 (sine) and TRIG2 (cosine) match each other, with respectively eight and
ten headings differing from the rounded approximation. Loading those exact
resources remains pending. These commands unblock retail
GANG.CST's walktopuppet placement. Diagnostic waitactorwalk observes a named
actor's motion record (0 absent, 1 present) with a bounded normal-session wait;
Penny's mousedown deliberately ignores clicks while her idle turn is active.

tools/verify-full-gym.ps1 passes cold boot through the boat-deck map jump,
GSTAIR1 -> VESTSTBD -> GYM, Penny's approach, the Zeitel movie and four explicit
briefing choices: 92 actions and seven total choices, mission=1 / phase=1.
build/full-gym.log and its native RGBA/PNG capture record the result. The demo's
306-action, twelve-choice winning route also passes with the stage context fix.
tools/verify-full-max.ps1 continues across DECKBD to Max, through CAFE's
Max/Will/Zeitel dialogue switches and the sailing movie, then collects the
pipe from CIGS.STG. The native cold-boot run passes 131 actions and ten total
choices, metzeitel=1 and pipe owned by Frank. build/full-max-pipe.log and its
RGBA/PNG capture record the result. tools/verify-full-mirror.ps1 continues via
the F-deck map to TURKSTRS/TURK, opens the water, examines the mirror and clicks
TURKMES.MOV's OK region: 167 actions and ten choices pass with mission=1,
phase=2, zeitclue=mirror and turkwater=0. build/full-mirror.log and its RGBA/PNG
capture record the result. tools/verify-full-control.ps1 continues through
STAIR2C/HALLF2C/TURB to CONTROL, accepts CSEA1's help request and enters the
rendered TURBINE.STG / Turbine 1: 232 actions and twelve choices pass from cold
boot. build/full-control.log and its RGBA/PNG capture record the result.
That run also verifies optional diagnostic dreamfactory_boot_skip_speech:
BootInput sends ordinary Escape events during active speech, throttled to one
per 100 ms to let the event queue drain. It is disabled by default and only
exists in the boot tracer. tools/trace-boot.ps1 accepts -SkipSpeech or the
DREAMFACTORY_BOOT_SKIP_SPEECH=1 environment setting. The earlier mirror run
used full speech. tools/verify-full-turbine.ps1 regulates the plant through
ordinary held mouse events: valve3 deg=7/value=36, pump1 deg=15/value=22,
slider deg=7/coal=68, then hold until electlag>=74. The authored exit gate and
engineer dialogue advance mission 1 to phase 3. All 252 actions and twelve
choices pass; build/full-turbine.log and its RGBA/PNG capture record the result.
Boot tracer gestures dragarc, dragpoint and holduntil emit pointer events only;
holduntil releases on a read-only global threshold or after 60 seconds.
expectpropdeg and expectglobalge observe the resulting state. Later retail
phases, Dust's remaining days, alternate demo outcomes and save/load still
require implementation or validation.

tools/verify-full-rubaiyat.ps1 passes 329 actions and twelve choices through
CONTROL -> ENGINE -> BOIL rooms 1/2/3 and recovery from coal4. The stash helper
passes 351 actions, placing the book in coal5 through INVEN1.STG's dobook loop,
with handitem empty and coalchute still coal4. clickthen schedules a second
physical mouse move/press after the first click has opened a modal interaction;
its delay is bounded to 200..10000 ms. No inventory state is injected.
build/full-rubaiyat.log and build/full-rubaiyat-stash.log and their captures
record the results.

tools/verify-full-vlad.ps1 climbs from chute five to Scene40/View45's upper
